<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Instruction of Amenemhat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Instruction of Amenemhat is an ancient Egyptian literary text of the Twelfth Dynasty in which the dead king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-i">Amenemhat I</a> advises his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i">Senusret I</a>, describing an attack on his life by members of his court. Probably composed early in Senusret's reign, it supported his rule and warned against trusting others. It was much copied by scribes in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/instruction-of-amenemhat.jpg" alt="Instruction of Amenemhat" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: rowanwindwhistler · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Facs%C3%ADmilLasInstruccionesDelReyAmenemhatI_(46149914632)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Also known as the Teaching of King Amenemhat to His Son Senusret, the work is a short poem of the sebayt genre, the Egyptian literature of instruction, written during the early Middle Kingdom. It may have been composed at Senusret's command, to praise his father and to support his own claim to the throne: a civil war is attested after Amenemhat's assassination, and Senusret had to suppress the forces ranged against him. Several centuries later the New Kingdom Papyrus Chester Beatty IV credited the poem to Kheti, called there the foremost of scribes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The poem is a dramatic monologue spoken by the ghost of the murdered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>. It opens by naming its author as the Osiris King of Upper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a>, Sehetepibre, the son of Re Amenemhat, and presents itself as advice to his son, the Horus Kheperkare, on keeping his kingship. The ghost warns Senusret to trust no one, neither friends nor brothers, since even those he had helped refused him support, and tells how his bodyguards killed him in his bed. He recalls his provisions for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, his military successes and his buildings, and the poem closes with blessings and advice on ruling as sole pharaoh.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The poem works as an apologia for the old king's reign and ends by urging Senusret to take the throne and rule wisely in his father's place. It is one of the earliest texts to refer to Asiatics. Later kings copied older writings to legitimise their own deeds, and the 25th Dynasty pharaoh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piye">Piye</a> took sections of the Instruction almost word for word. The poem was still read in the fifth century BCE, about 1500 years after it was written, and it was copied into Greek as the Teaching of King Ammenemes I to His Son Sesostris I.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Instructions of Amenemhat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Royal ideology was spread through literature: the <b>Instruction of Amenemhat</b>, in which the dead king warns his son after an attack on his life, and the Loyalist Instruction taught service to the crown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
